        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nI do get the point, but I am afraid technical details make this almost impossible. The problem is that there needs to be a separate buffer filled with text in order to highlight it. Both LSP and tree-sitter highlighting are designed as a result of *something* being attached to the buffer and react to changes in it.\n\nWhile 'mini.diff' overlay is implemented via virtual lines that are displayed directly above/below the line. These lines have to be manually constructed from highlighted parts: i.e. provide something like `{ { 'text1', 'hl_group1' }, { 'text2', 'hl_group2' } }`. Each part can be highlighted with only a single highlight group, while tree-sitter and LSP might require a combination of several highlight groups (like foreground from one group, background from the other, underline from the third one).\n\n*In theory*, there is `vim.inspect_pos()` that can return a list of \"active\" highlight groups at certain position in the buffer. *In reality*, both LSP and tree-sitter highlighting are designed to be shown only when the buffer is visible. So having some \"hidden\" buffer with reference text and attached LSP/tree-sitter while utilizing `vim.inspect_pos()` is probably impossible (I've just tried it and no luck).\n\nI'll ponder a bit about this, but I am pretty sure that having a dedicated syntax highlighting in overlay's virtual lines will not be added.",
