        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Considering CodeDiff and DiffView provide this feature, I am wondering if this is now more feasible. I currently keep installed CodeDiff only for this reason.\n\nIt looks like 'esmuellert/codediff.nvim/' [parses each set of lines separately](https://github.com/esmuellert/codediff.nvim/blob/8afc229d38dc13ce71b2ffbb860084b2c726e061/lua/codediff/ui/inline.lua#L14-L211) and then builds virtual lines from those chunks. This is a reasonable approach, but unfortunately this still doesn't fit the reasoning in [this comment](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1847#issuecomment-3008216021). I.e. it is both not concise and not efficient way of showing highlighting in virtual lines.\n\nThis is a good approach for something that has predictable line size and does not need to run constantly in the background. Like in a new \"ui2\" command line in Neovim>=0.12 this same approach provides tree-sitter highlighting of the input Vimscript command. It is known to be a single line/chunk and does not have to be run all the time when editing any buffer.",
